<GuideArticleLayout
  title="Choosing a box without wasting space"
  description="A practical way to think about internal dimensions, fit, empty space and why the smallest-looking box is not always the best packing decision."
  category="Box selection"
  readTime="6 min read"
>
  <h2>Start with usable space, not the box label</h2>

  <p>
    A carton can look large enough and still be a poor fit.
    What matters for packing is the usable space inside the
    carton and whether the item can be placed there in an
    allowed orientation.
  </p>

  <p>
    That is why Packmetry treats <strong>internal carton
    dimensions</strong> as the geometry used for placement.
    External package dimensions have a different purpose:
    they matter when dimensional-weight analysis is enabled.
  </p>

  <div class="pm-guide-note">
    <strong>Useful distinction</strong>
    <p>
      Internal dimensions answer “will it fit?” External
      dimensions answer questions about the outside package,
      including dimensional-weight calculations.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Do not optimize empty space in isolation</h2>

  <p>
    A lower empty-volume number is useful, but it is not the
    only thing that can make one plan better than another.
    The number of cartons, available inventory, supported
    handling rules and your selected packing objective can
    all change the preferred result.
  </p>

  <p>
    For an everyday shipment, a practical decision often
    starts with a simpler question: can all requested items
    be packed into fewer cartons without violating the
    dimensions and constraints you entered?
  </p>

  <h3>When two boxes both fit</h3>

  <p>
    If two candidate cartons both produce verified plans,
    empty space becomes a useful tie-breaker. A tighter plan
    usually means less unused internal volume, but you should
    still leave any real-world cushioning or spacing needs in
    the inputs rather than assuming a perfectly tight pack.
  </p>

  <h2>Rotation can change the answer</h2>

  <p>
    The same item can fit in very different ways depending
    on its rotation policy. An unrestricted item may use any
    solver-supported orientation. An upright or fixed item
    has fewer valid placements, which can require a larger
    carton even when raw volume suggests otherwise.
  </p>

  <p>
    Volume alone cannot prove that an item fits. Geometry
    matters because the length, width and height still need
    to occupy valid positions inside the carton.
  </p>

  <h2>Use the verified arrangement as the source of truth</h2>

  <p>
    Packmetry does not treat the 3D view as a decorative
    reconstruction. The visualization follows the canonical
    verified placement plan. That means the result summary
    and the 3D arrangement refer to the same packing result.
  </p>

  <p>
    A good box decision is therefore not simply “pick the
    smallest dimensions.” It is: choose a carton that
    produces a verified plan under the constraints that
    actually matter for the job.
  </p>
</GuideArticleLayout>
